# Documentation Progress

## Project Information

- **Course:** COS Project & Portfolio 1
- **Student:** Nelson
- **Project:** Micare - Cathedral of Lies
- **Language:** C++17
- **Final Build:** Console application

## Project Overview

Micare began as a small multi-file C++ project and grew into a complete console RPG with exploration, investigations, combat, progression, equipment, Shimmer abilities, Artifacts, bosses, room progression, and repeatable post-clear encounters.

Throughout the course, the project went through several major structural changes. Early development focused on building the core classes and gameplay loop. A graphical Win32 version was briefly developed, but the assessed project was later converted into a standard C++17 console application and reorganized inside the required `dev` folder.

The final version focuses on a complete Floor I experience while preserving the larger Micare concept for future development.

---

## Initial Project Foundation

The first development pass established the basic architecture of the game.

Completed work included:

- Organized the project into `include/` and `src/` folders.
- Created the core `Player`, `Enemy`, `Item`, `Clue`, `Area`, and `ShimmerSkill` classes.
- Added `MapManager`, `ProgressionManager`, `EncounterManager`, and `GameManager`.
- Used `std::unique_ptr` for owned runtime objects such as enemies.
- Added references and non-owning pointers where managers needed access to existing objects.
- Added XP and level progression.
- Added a basic combat system.
- Added inventory and clue storage.
- Added deterministic room generation.
- Created a playable console loop.
- Configured the project for C++17.
- Completed the first successful build and gameplay smoke test.

This stage established the class structure and relationships that the rest of the project was built on.

